// Haan, JavaScript me Hoisting ko simple way me samjho 👇

// Hoisting kya hoti hai?

// Hoisting ka matlab hai JavaScript execution se pehle declarations ko process karti hai, jiski wajah se kuch variables/functions ko code me declaration se pehle bhi access kiya ja sakta hai.

// Lekin var, let, const aur functions ke case me behaviour alag hota hai.

// 1. var — hoist hota hai
// console.log(a);

// var a = 10;

// Output:

// undefined

// JavaScript internally roughly is tarah treat karti hai:

// var a;

// console.log(a);

// a = 10;

// Isliye error nahi aaya, bas undefined mila.

// 2. let — hoist hota hai, but access nahi kar sakte
// console.log(a);

// let a = 10;

// ❌ Error:

// ReferenceError

// Same const ke saath:

// console.log(a);

// const a = 10;

// ❌ ReferenceError

// let aur const declaration se lekar initialization tak Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) me hote hain.

// 3. Function declaration

// Function declarations ko bhi hoist kiya jata hai:

// hello();

// function hello() {
//     console.log("Hello!");
// }

// Output:

// Hello!

// Ye kaam karta hai. ✅

// Ek table yaad kar lo 🧠
// Declaration	Hoisted?	Declaration se pehle access?
// var	✅	✅ → undefined
// let	✅	❌ TDZ
// const	✅	❌ TDZ
// Function declaration	✅	✅

// Exam/interview wali one-liner:

// Hoisting is JavaScript's behavior where declarations are processed before the code is executed.

// Aur ek important point: hoisting ka matlab literally code ko upar move karna nahi hai. Ye JavaScript ke creation/execution phases ko samajhne ka simplified way hai.